the report author matt parr and good to  talk to you today the independent report  last summer did conclude that the met  was institutionally racist and  that the met had put up a huge number of  barriers to their report  why did you not find it to be  institutionally corrupt  well good afternoon um we found an awful  lot wrong with it let's be clear and  there's  huge areas of agreement between us and  the panel  and as you said in europe  uh the met's got an awful lot to do to  get its anti-corruption practices to  where they should be i think where the  slight difference was and bearing in  mind we had different remits  as we simply didn't see evidence that  the mistakes amit had made  were dishonest or were sort of  coordinated uh in a campaign to  frustrate the the panels uh work um we  saw lots of things that went wrong and  there are lots of things we criticized  and met for uh but i think without that  element of deliberate dishonesty uh it's  not a phrase that we'd use to call them  institutionally corrupt  that doesn't mean there isn't an awful  lot wrong and it's a very critical  report  well indeed and let's look at some of  those those things that are wrong uh the  mets employing criminals employing  people who've committed crimes why does  the police force do this  well i think every case should be judged  on its merits there might be occasions  where  someone who's had some sort of  transgression a few years beforehand  relatively minor should be allowed to  become a police officer uh we do think  that the mets tolerance for things like  that and their risk appetite as they  called it  is too high but where we're more  concerned is if you are going to take  somebody like that in and there might be  reasons to do so um it's a higher risk  and therefore you ought to monitor it  carefully and they ought to supervise it  more carefully and there's not enough  evidence of that going on uh  and in fact all sorts of degrees of  supervision of people who have  associations with criminals or have got  a checkered past  and nowhere near they ought to be  i mean why is this happening that that  these people are not then being  monitored once they're into the force  i mean like you i  sort of look at it and wonder how on  earth  after 35 years of criticism over  daniel morgan the way they handled the  initial investigation the subsequent  investigations the failures in court  in which police corruption was a very  major factor uh we find it uh  bewildering uh that no one seems to have  said this is something that we can't put  up with we've got to get it right uh  we've got to be absolutely sure over  this now  doing all the things that we've  recommended and recommended 20  areas for improvement  doing all those things doesn't guarantee  that you never get any corruption of  force but i think it's the minimum that  the public would expect is that the met  should be doing all it can to minimize  that and at the moment it just isn't  so you know essentially it almost seems  to what you're finding is that there  isn't sort of an institutional  conspiracy to be corrupt  but but there is corruption just by by  the fault of almost indifference  uh indifference is a word we use in the  report it's what it looks like to us uh  i'm sure the met will say differently  but uh there were low standards in the  way they looked after property which  again was a  feature with the exhibits of the daniel  morgan case there were poor standards in  the way they supervise people some of  their policies are not in accordance  with what the national guidance says  and there were just you know  people not doing the right things to  minimize the risk of corruption almost  across the board now there were some  pockets of real excellence so one of the  things we congratulate the met for is  that they had a big betting vetting  backlog they had 16 000 people unvetted  uh three or four years ago and they've  pretty much eradicated that and the real  high profile uh serious cases they  investigate those really well  but it's all the other things that they  ought to do uh to to make sure the wrong  people don't join the met and if they do  to make sure that they're found out and  they can't get away with corrupt  behavior it's all those little things  that they ought to be doing uh that that  on their own don't look as important but  in some uh they present a picture for us  of a force that doesn't really take it  as seriously as it should especially  after what's gone wrong in in the daniel  morgan case  what do you think it says about an  organization that they're not keeping  track of of warrant cards and how has  access codes for security things etched  on doors  yeah i mean it says something about the  culture of the organization doesn't it i  mean we've we've all seen these dreadful  uh cases where misogyny sexism racism  has  has blackened the met's reputation uh  but i think you know as an organization  you look at culture wider than that it's  it's how people behave when no one's  looking it's surely the first person to  see that there's this security breach  should pick it up and correct it uh  surely it's everybody should know that  if there's somebody who's got uh uh a  declarable association with somebody who  has to be related to someone who's an  active criminal  that should be supervised and people  should be aware and they're just not so  i think it speaks of an organization  that hasn't really got a grip  on this particularly important part of  police culture and practice  very interesting to talk to you thanks  very much for joining us there matt parr  h inspector of constabulary's carryouts  report into the daniel morgan case
 

